In article <UZO=ekG00WB5ITrUtZ@andrew.cmu.edu> dh07+@andrew.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:

>you "a"pply a credit card.
>in what direction? [hjklyubn.]

>if the adjacent object in the indicated direction is a shopkeeper
>    in a shop then you could go into debt if you're not already in debt
>    and purchase certain unpaid items that you choose from your inventory.

I like this a lot but with a "credit limit" of 1000*your_exp_level.

>if the indicated direction is "." (i.e. yourself) you could use your
>    charge card to restore expended charges in appropriate magic. this
>    would involve the destruction of the card, as well. there's a chance
>    that the card would not work as reliably as a scroll of charging.

I don't like this much.

>there are some features associated with debt:
>you cannot go further into debt if you are already in debt, the
>    transaction would be refused.

I think a credit limit should be used instead.

>you cannot buy other things at this or any other shop until you get out
>    of debt. you can look and, of course, you could steal.

Rephrase that as: you cannot buy other things on credit at this or any other
shop while your balance exceeds your credit limit.

>credit involves some overhead so your debt is greater than the amount you
>    purchased. how, where and when to repay are explained to you before
>    you go into debt and you must agree to those terms or the transaction
>    is refused. the "p"ay command is used to repay.

I suggest a 10% finance charge plus every N (1000?) turns an additional
10% is added to your debt.

>if your debt was $100 gold pieces you would have to repay $150 gold pieces
>    at the original shop to get out of debt. optionally you would have to
>    pay $200 gold pieces at any other shop (or temple!) to get out of
>    debt. you don't have to repay in full at any one time but you must
>    be repaid in full on time to avoid the human, @, "repo-man".

I think you should be able to pay debt anywhere.  The "overhead" would
already be included in the debt.

> [stuff about repo-men]

I like it!

